  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Thursday)\nIssued at 1159 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nBroad mid-level ridging builds into the central CONUS on Friday \nand persists through the weekend as expansive surface high \npressure settles over the Southern Plains. Global deterministic \nmodels and ensemble suites show strong agreement on a dry and \ntranquil weather pattern prevailing through early to mid-next \nweek. \n\nHigh temperatures on Friday will be highly dependent on cloud \ncover, especially north of I-40. Low-level stratus will be \nadvecting in from the north Friday morning, reaching the I-40 \ncorridor around midday before mixing gradually breaks the cloud \ncover up through the afternoon. With the cloud cover, temperature\nmay struggle to get out of the 50s for most areas across \nnortheast Oklahoma and far northwest Arkansas, whereas \ntemperatures will rise into the mid to upper 60s where there is\nless cloud coverage. Temperatures on Saturday will be quite \nseasonable, topping out in the lower to mid-60s beneath partly \ncloudy/partly sunny skies. Overnight lows Friday night and \nSaturday night will fall into the mid to upper 30s across typical\nvalley locations and low 40s elsewhere. A few locations may \nbriefly flirt with freezing temperatures. Patchy frost may develop\nboth nights.\n\nBy Sunday into the middle of next week, the surface ridge axis \nshifts east toward the Ohio Valley, re-establishing southerly \nreturn flow across eastern Oklahoma and northwest Arkansas. This \nwill support a gradual moderating trend, with afternoon highs \nclimbing back into the upper 60s to mid 70s Monday through next \nThursday. Operational and ensemble NBM PoPs remain virtually zero \nCWA-wide through the end of the forecast period, with no hazardous\nweather systems on the horizon.\n\nMejia",
